Exactly. The name preserves the uncertainty.

Fishing means you can choose the water, bait, timing, technique, patience, and number of casts. Catching is an outcome you influence but do not command.

That distinction is bigger than fishing: process is yours; result is negotiated with reality.

A lot of good systems are built that way:

* farming, not harvesting
* courting, not possessing
* searching, not finding
* investing, not profiting
* learning, not knowing

You can design the game to increase your odds, but if you rename the activity after the reward, you start confusing skillful participation with guaranteed control.

That is probably one of the cleaner antidotes to outcome obsession: cast well, read the water, update from misses, and keep fishing.